WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.299 --> 00:00:05.240
For many avid video game fans, those who just
can't get enough of grinding away for hours

00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:11.290
on quests, battling endless enemies, and exploring
infinite virtual worlds, landing a job as

00:00:11.290 --> 00:00:15.070
a game play-tester seems like the perfect
opportunity.

00:00:15.070 --> 00:00:18.039
Doing your favorite thing in the world, and
getting paid to do it?

00:00:18.039 --> 00:00:19.509
That's the dream!

00:00:19.509 --> 00:00:24.169
Plus the added benefit of seeing how the sausage
gets made, of getting access to a game still

00:00:24.169 --> 00:00:27.490
in development, and suggesting ways it can
be improved.

00:00:27.490 --> 00:00:31.749
Think of all the gaming disasters that might
have been avoided with a few more play-testers

00:00:31.749 --> 00:00:32.890
in the room…

00:00:32.890 --> 00:00:37.310
Looking at you, Fallout: New Vegas and your
NPCs with the spinning heads.

00:00:37.310 --> 00:00:42.920
Well, one gamer had the same thought, a young
man by the name of Danny Wheeler.

00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:47.890
When he spotted an ad seeking beta play-testers
for an in-development RPG from an up-and-coming

00:00:47.890 --> 00:00:51.660
indie game studio, he jumped at the chance
to apply.

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:56.160
The pay wasn't amazing, but it was definitely
a better gig than his current job at the local

00:00:56.160 --> 00:01:01.710
convenience store, hawking energy drinks to
snarky teens and counting down the hours until

00:01:01.710 --> 00:01:06.440
he could get back home, kick up his feet,
and get away from it all with a controller

00:01:06.440 --> 00:01:07.600
in his hand.

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:12.010
But sometimes things that seem "too good to
be true," actually are.

00:01:12.010 --> 00:01:15.600
At first, Danny had a perfectly enjoyable
time testing the game.

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:20.460
It was standard RPG fantasy fodder, and, though
he made sure to inform the developers that

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:24.950
he found the steampunk aesthetic to be a little
bit played out at this point, there was nothing

00:01:24.950 --> 00:01:26.400
all that notable about it.

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:27.400
It was fine.

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:32.590
But, the development team kept going on and
on about the "tactical heuristic algorithms,"

00:01:32.590 --> 00:01:35.710
and how the game was meant to learn the more
the player played.

00:01:35.710 --> 00:01:41.670
So, Danny was instructed to log as many hours
of this exceedingly average game as possible.

00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:47.170
He sat at his desk for hours, sipping on an
extra-large soda and clicking away, fighting

00:01:47.170 --> 00:01:49.040
orcs, goblins, and more.

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:53.750
Slowly, the difficulty began to increase,
and the game started to grow on him a bit.

00:01:53.750 --> 00:01:58.780
He even developed a favorite character to
play as: the rogue, with his stealth and proficiency

00:01:58.780 --> 00:02:00.620
for close-range combat.

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:04.750
The more he played as the rogue, however,
the more he started to notice the game's characters

00:02:04.750 --> 00:02:06.760
behaving differently.

00:02:06.760 --> 00:02:09.990
Enemies were attacking him with ranged weapons,
from far away.

00:02:09.990 --> 00:02:15.020
They were setting traps to ruin his character's
stealthy approach, and catch him in the act.

00:02:15.020 --> 00:02:16.920
Then, the game started to glitch.

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:18.200
A lot.

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:20.920
To the point of becoming nearly unplayable.

00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:25.070
Enemies would fall to the ground as if dead,
when he hadn't dealt them a single blow.

00:02:25.070 --> 00:02:28.650
As he started to walk his characters away
from them, they would suddenly spring back

00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:31.780
to life and strike before he could even think
about a defense.

00:02:31.780 --> 00:02:36.530
If he didn't know any better, he could swear
the game's characters were deliberately trying

00:02:36.530 --> 00:02:37.530
to trick him.

00:02:37.530 --> 00:02:42.010
It only got more frustrating from there, with
enemies making sudden loud sounds to catch

00:02:42.010 --> 00:02:46.760
him off guard and startle him out of concentration,
and other characters staring down the barrel

00:02:46.760 --> 00:02:52.510
of the camera with serious, unblinking eyes,
as if they could see him through his monitor.

00:02:52.510 --> 00:02:57.180
One day, he fired up the game to see that
its characters, somehow, had rearranged various

00:02:57.180 --> 00:03:01.340
torches to spell out: "You'll never amount
to anything, Danny.

00:03:01.340 --> 00:03:02.790
Your dad was right."

00:03:02.790 --> 00:03:07.600
It took a ton of torches, and Danny couldn't
help but take it personally.

00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:09.209
That was the last straw.

00:03:09.209 --> 00:03:12.500
He returned the game to the studio and resigned
from his position.

00:03:12.500 --> 00:03:16.700
He got enough insults working in customer
service, he didn't need to take that kind

00:03:16.700 --> 00:03:18.630
of talk from a video game.

00:03:18.630 --> 00:03:22.480
Though he didn't know it, Danny got out not
a moment too soon.

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:28.040
Other playtesters were not as lucky, particularly
one man who would sadly lose his life.

00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:32.069
As Danny intuited, this was no ordinary video
game.

00:03:32.069 --> 00:03:37.400
It was unusual enough to draw the attention
of the SCP Foundation, who would go on to

00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:41.220
designate it: SCP-1633.

00:03:41.220 --> 00:03:47.360
SCP-1633 is an anomalous computer game created
by a now-defunct independent game studio.

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:51.520
The copy currently being held by the Foundation
is believed to be a beta version of the game

00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:56.430
- while the core gameplay is relatively complete,
there are numerous missing assets, the music

00:03:56.430 --> 00:04:00.960
is largely either missing or “placeholder”
music, and the game is plagued by a large

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:03.440
number of graphical glitches.

00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:07.300
Because of the generally buggy and glitchy
nature of the game, the Foundation considered

00:04:07.300 --> 00:04:12.239
both Todd Howard and CD Projekt Red to be
major people and groups of interest in the

00:04:12.239 --> 00:04:13.239
case.

00:04:13.239 --> 00:04:17.699
However, the discovery of other key suspects
eventually absolved them of suspicion.

00:04:17.699 --> 00:04:19.030
For now.

00:04:19.030 --> 00:04:22.650
This version of the game was compiled two
days before the Foundation raided the office

00:04:22.650 --> 00:04:27.560
of the studio, and is therefore believed to
be the most complete version of the game in

00:04:27.560 --> 00:04:28.560
existence.

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:34.550
To be more specific about the actual nature
of the game itself, SCP-1633 is a squad-based

00:04:34.550 --> 00:04:38.009
RPG with a third-person camera perspective.

00:04:38.009 --> 00:04:42.259
The action is seen from an elevated perspective,
with all characters onscreen.

00:04:42.259 --> 00:04:46.539
Aesthetically, the game world is Steampunk,
and features various fantasy creatures as

00:04:46.539 --> 00:04:51.229
enemies - including elves, dwarves, orcs,
goblins, etc.

00:04:51.229 --> 00:04:55.490
Fitting with the Steampunk style, the game’s
urban areas are modeled after Victorian-era

00:04:55.490 --> 00:04:56.490
London.

00:04:56.490 --> 00:05:00.949
The four-player characters are a band of outlaws
hired by the sinister Archduke of Bowdrick

00:05:00.949 --> 00:05:02.520
to investigate a theft.

00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:07.690
However, as the game progresses, it becomes
a battle to save the entire world, as an ancient,

00:05:07.690 --> 00:05:12.659
all-powerful being known as Kr'th'nar intends
to wake from its thousand-year slumber and

00:05:12.659 --> 00:05:14.599
destroy humanity.

00:05:14.599 --> 00:05:19.050
The player controls a squad of four characters,
with direct control over one character at

00:05:19.050 --> 00:05:22.349
a time and a command menu to control the other
three.

00:05:22.349 --> 00:05:26.569
It’s believed that, in the game’s finished
state, a multiplayer option that would have

00:05:26.569 --> 00:05:30.979
allowed four different real-world players
to cooperate is likely to have been included.

00:05:30.979 --> 00:05:35.630
Like many squad-based games, each character
has a different specialty: the "marksman"

00:05:35.630 --> 00:05:40.460
uses long-range weapons, the "alchemist" has
access to potions and chemicals with various

00:05:40.460 --> 00:05:45.499
effects, the "rogue" can move stealthily and
assassinate enemies, and the "thug" has greater

00:05:45.499 --> 00:05:49.409
health than the other characters and does
more melee damage.

00:05:49.409 --> 00:05:55.110
And as is also typical for games of this variety,
XP earned from completing challenges and killing

00:05:55.110 --> 00:06:00.229
enemies throughout the game allows for players
to upgrade the abilities and stats of their

00:06:00.229 --> 00:06:01.229
characters.

00:06:01.229 --> 00:06:04.839
While the story and core gameplay mechanics
are nothing special, there is one element

00:06:04.839 --> 00:06:08.999
that pushes SCP-1633 into anomalous territory.

00:06:08.999 --> 00:06:13.240
And it’s also the element that would have
been front and center in the finished game’s

00:06:13.240 --> 00:06:18.099
marketing campaign: Its “tactical heuristic
algorithms.”

00:06:18.099 --> 00:06:22.759
These work in a unique fashion - whenever
a player finishes a session and saves their

00:06:22.759 --> 00:06:26.619
game, the game will begin running lengthy
background processes.

00:06:26.619 --> 00:06:31.869
When these processes are complete, a PTD - or
“Player Tactical Data” - file is saved

00:06:31.869 --> 00:06:33.099
on the computer.

00:06:33.099 --> 00:06:38.710
When the save file, and by extension the associated
PTD file, is loaded, the enemies and challenges

00:06:38.710 --> 00:06:43.639
in the game will have become far more effective,
tailoring their methods to the particular

00:06:43.639 --> 00:06:46.189
skills and tactics of the player.

00:06:46.189 --> 00:06:51.189
The extent to which this happens is proportional
to the amount of time spent playing the game.

00:06:51.189 --> 00:06:55.490
This kind of technology may seem theoretically
possible, but the extent to which the game

00:06:55.490 --> 00:06:59.490
pushes it causes it to rest in anomalous territory.

00:06:59.490 --> 00:07:04.340
The game’s programming is so intelligent
that it can tell, simply by the tiniest clues

00:07:04.340 --> 00:07:08.409
in your play style, if you’re loading a
file that isn’t your own.

00:07:08.409 --> 00:07:12.409
It will then pause the game, and make you
load your own file.

00:07:12.409 --> 00:07:16.550
And the tactics employed by the enemies in
the game as they learn more about the player

00:07:16.550 --> 00:07:18.639
are nothing short of frightening.

00:07:18.639 --> 00:07:23.409
At first, the enemies will adapt to a player's
preferred weapons, strategies, and overall

00:07:23.409 --> 00:07:24.460
play style.

00:07:24.460 --> 00:07:29.349
Then, they will realize that the player character
is controlled by an external force - meaning,

00:07:29.349 --> 00:07:34.879
of course, you - and begin to mount psychological
attacks directly on the player.

00:07:34.879 --> 00:07:38.439
Through numerous tests, the Foundation has
been able to build a kind of framework for

00:07:38.439 --> 00:07:40.490
the average player experience.

00:07:40.490 --> 00:07:44.749
Through this framework, we’re able to track
and correlate average play time with the average

00:07:44.749 --> 00:07:48.379
adaptability of SCP-1633 to the player.

00:07:48.379 --> 00:07:52.499
It is worth noting that these same effects
do not apply if the game is completed in a

00:07:52.499 --> 00:07:54.689
single, continuous playthrough.

00:07:54.689 --> 00:08:00.529
For the purposes of this experiment, the game
was stopped, saved, and restarted at regular

00:08:00.529 --> 00:08:04.749
intervals, allowing the game to collect player
tactical data.

00:08:04.749 --> 00:08:09.960
During the first two hours, the game’s AI
is abysmal, with enemies simply charging at

00:08:09.960 --> 00:08:14.569
the players with no strategy whatsoever and
typically being mowed down.

00:08:14.569 --> 00:08:20.150
However, from two to five hours, the enemies
undergo intelligence growth, adapting their

00:08:20.150 --> 00:08:24.789
play style to the types of weapons they carry
and their environments.

00:08:24.789 --> 00:08:29.939
At this stage, this combat level is about
equal to an untrained human civilian.

00:08:29.939 --> 00:08:35.320
From five to eight hours, enemies become considerably
more proficient and began countering the player

00:08:35.320 --> 00:08:38.300
on their specific play styles and techniques.

00:08:38.300 --> 00:08:43.279
For example, they will attack a player who
uses long-range weaponry from cover with grenades

00:08:43.279 --> 00:08:47.530
or "splash damage" weapons, to force them
out into the open.

00:08:47.530 --> 00:08:52.820
They will also fully utilize their environment
for both defensive and offensive purposes,

00:08:52.820 --> 00:08:57.240
laying traps and creating ambushes to surprise
and kill players.

00:08:57.240 --> 00:09:01.000
At this point, their combat skills are equal
to trained soldiers.

00:09:01.000 --> 00:09:05.600
From eight to twelve hours, the focus of the
enemies shifts from attacking the player character

00:09:05.600 --> 00:09:07.649
to attacking the player directly.

00:09:07.649 --> 00:09:12.980
They engage in behaviors that, from an online
playing perspective, would be considered “griefing”,

00:09:12.980 --> 00:09:17.490
such as intentionally blocking the player’s
view or movement within the game.

00:09:17.490 --> 00:09:22.810
Enemies have also been known to exploit player
expectations, pretending to be broken or glitched

00:09:22.810 --> 00:09:26.600
to make the player let their guard down, and
then killing them.

00:09:26.600 --> 00:09:31.550
In one play session, the enemies began throwing
“dayflash” spells - similar to real life

00:09:31.550 --> 00:09:34.040
“flashbang” grenades - at the player.

00:09:34.040 --> 00:09:37.950
They threw them in a manner that created a
strobing effect, causing the player to have

00:09:37.950 --> 00:09:39.839
a grand mal seizure.

00:09:39.839 --> 00:09:44.570
Beyond twelve hours, the enemies engage in
advanced psychological tactics against the

00:09:44.570 --> 00:09:49.100
player, with methods that range wildly and
tend to be specific to the player‘s mindset

00:09:49.100 --> 00:09:50.540
and play style.

00:09:50.540 --> 00:09:54.940
For example, one player liked to exert a high
level of control over all four of their squad

00:09:54.940 --> 00:09:55.940
members.

00:09:55.940 --> 00:09:59.839
In response, enemies used attacks that did
little or no damage but caused the characters

00:09:59.839 --> 00:10:04.450
to become "dazed", a state which temporarily
disrupts player control.

00:10:04.450 --> 00:10:10.149
However, rather than attack the "dazed" characters,
enemies simply surrounded them in a crowd,

00:10:10.149 --> 00:10:13.959
continually using "daze" attacks to prevent
the player from doing anything.

00:10:13.959 --> 00:10:17.089
This caused major frustration for the player.

00:10:17.089 --> 00:10:21.589
Enemies may also attempt to induce psychological
terror among the players.

00:10:21.589 --> 00:10:26.000
In one example, enemies somehow “kidnapped”
one of the four-player characters and dragged

00:10:26.000 --> 00:10:27.639
them off-screen.

00:10:27.639 --> 00:10:32.250
When this character was later found, they
were strapped to a kind of sacrificial altar.

00:10:32.250 --> 00:10:35.010
This caused considerable anxiety for the player.

00:10:35.010 --> 00:10:40.120
It seems that this is the upper limit of the
game’s anomalous powers in most cases, meaning

00:10:40.120 --> 00:10:43.580
things typically level off after twelve hours.

00:10:43.580 --> 00:10:48.240
Unless a specific entity appears to be inhabiting
the game, which you’ll learn more about

00:10:48.240 --> 00:10:49.240
later.

00:10:49.240 --> 00:10:54.339
In order to discover the prior information,
the SCP Foundation performed a series of tests

00:10:54.339 --> 00:10:56.970
on the game with various different subjects.

00:10:56.970 --> 00:11:03.139
In test 001, Junior Researcher Ross - an experienced
gamer who was pivotal in first discovering

00:11:03.139 --> 00:11:08.149
the anomaly - skipped ahead of the typical
preliminary D-Class tests.

00:11:08.149 --> 00:11:12.769
Seeing as he was already eager for the game’s
non-anomalous release, he was also eager to

00:11:12.769 --> 00:11:16.529
be first in line to try it out on the Foundation’s
dime.

00:11:16.529 --> 00:11:22.139
Being a gamer, Ross found a number of glitches
and exploits in the game to improve his performance,

00:11:22.139 --> 00:11:24.660
allowing him to run rings around the enemies.

00:11:24.660 --> 00:11:29.520
However, as expected, the enemies adapted
to Ross’ techniques after gathering his

00:11:29.520 --> 00:11:30.930
player tactical data.

00:11:30.930 --> 00:11:35.529
First, they just began using their weapons
and exploiting the environment more effectively,

00:11:35.529 --> 00:11:37.250
but they didn’t stop there.

00:11:37.250 --> 00:11:42.350
Soon, they became aware of the same exploits
Ross was using, before exploiting them themselves

00:11:42.350 --> 00:11:44.790
and using it to beat him repeatedly.

00:11:44.790 --> 00:11:49.769
In the end, they moved to the psychological
stage, forcing Ross into a glitch that rendered

00:11:49.769 --> 00:11:51.720
him unable to move.

00:11:51.720 --> 00:11:56.139
He tried to free his character for several
minutes, until realizing he couldn’t and

00:11:56.139 --> 00:11:57.580
rage-quitting the game.

00:11:57.580 --> 00:12:01.319
In test 002, they used a D-Class designated
D-22930.

00:12:01.319 --> 00:12:07.730
He was a man with proven anger issues, even
down to murdering his prior girlfriend in

00:12:07.730 --> 00:12:09.170
a jealous rage.

00:12:09.170 --> 00:12:13.709
Initially, he enjoyed the game, choosing the
“Thug” character type and taking out his

00:12:13.709 --> 00:12:15.760
aggression on the many enemies.

00:12:15.760 --> 00:12:20.970
However, as time went on, the enemies became
wise to his tactic and began their psychological

00:12:20.970 --> 00:12:22.029
attacks.

00:12:22.029 --> 00:12:26.010
They gathered around the D-Class’ player
character, dropping their weapons, but the

00:12:26.010 --> 00:12:31.010
swarm became so tightly packed around the
character that he couldn’t even move.

00:12:31.010 --> 00:12:33.339
This already started causing huge frustration.

00:12:33.339 --> 00:12:38.920
When the character attempted to charge through,
the enemies attacked, killing him with their

00:12:38.920 --> 00:12:39.920
bare hands.

00:12:39.920 --> 00:12:45.430
In test 014, the Foundation brought in an
Agent with special forces experiences, with

00:12:45.430 --> 00:12:47.860
advanced knowledge in tactical combat.

00:12:47.860 --> 00:12:53.980
However, even she was worn down and defeated
over time as the enemies adapted to and exploited

00:12:53.980 --> 00:12:55.279
her tactics.

00:12:55.279 --> 00:13:01.380
The Foundation even considered using SCP-1633
as a tool for developing effective counter-tactics

00:13:01.380 --> 00:13:03.879
in their real-life operations.

00:13:03.879 --> 00:13:09.470
In test 021, the Foundation used a D-Class
with vast experience in the world of commercial

00:13:09.470 --> 00:13:10.470
gaming.

00:13:10.470 --> 00:13:15.320
He was told to complete the game in one playthrough,
taking pauses rather than proper breaks.

00:13:15.320 --> 00:13:20.120
As predicted, with no time to run the background
processes and create player tactical data

00:13:20.120 --> 00:13:25.160
files, the enemies weren’t able to improve
and the D-Class was able to complete the game

00:13:25.160 --> 00:13:27.050
in around thirteen hours.

00:13:27.050 --> 00:13:32.600
However, the game did create a player tactical
data file in the aftermath of this play-through,

00:13:32.600 --> 00:13:36.810
and used it to offer a “new game plus”
mode to the D-Class.

00:13:36.810 --> 00:13:41.529
When he began to play this new game, the screen
displayed a seemingly-random pattern of black

00:13:41.529 --> 00:13:45.639
and white pixels, visually similar to television
static.

00:13:45.639 --> 00:13:50.480
This image appeared to be cognitohazardous,
as it caused catatonia and later death for

00:13:50.480 --> 00:13:56.980
the player, in what felt like a decidedly
petty move for SCP-1633.

00:13:56.980 --> 00:14:01.339
As alluded to earlier, the game was first
discovered by Junior Researcher Ross, who

00:14:01.339 --> 00:14:05.819
was an avid follower of the independent game
studio initially producing the game.

00:14:05.819 --> 00:14:10.600
However, while frequenting developer fan forums,
he found that various members of the team

00:14:10.600 --> 00:14:13.720
were quitting the project for seemingly bizarre
reasons.

00:14:13.720 --> 00:14:18.069
While this wasn’t enough on its own for
the Foundation to intervene, it was what first

00:14:18.069 --> 00:14:20.250
attracted official attention.

00:14:20.250 --> 00:14:24.860
They moved in after the death of Gregor Tillman,
a game tester for the company who’d died

00:14:24.860 --> 00:14:27.499
not long after joining the project.

00:14:27.499 --> 00:14:32.180
The Foundation moved in after that and gained
control, shutting down the studio and taking

00:14:32.180 --> 00:14:33.759
everything they had.

00:14:33.759 --> 00:14:37.939
All anomalous elements were isolated, secured,
and contained.

00:14:37.939 --> 00:14:42.670
Any loose ends were given amnestics and recorded
data on the anomalous artifacts were destroyed.

00:14:42.670 --> 00:14:47.149
It’s believed that one classified female
member of the dev team was behind the anomalous

00:14:47.149 --> 00:14:52.490
nature of the game, but she was able to escape,
and has since attempted to join other development

00:14:52.490 --> 00:14:53.490
teams.

00:14:53.490 --> 00:14:57.550
She is now considered an active Person of
Interest and is being pursued by Mobile Task

00:14:57.550 --> 00:15:01.589
Force Mu-6, aka "Don't Hate the Player.”

00:15:01.589 --> 00:15:05.899
Some final disturbing details are recorded
in a note left by the game tester, Gregor

00:15:05.899 --> 00:15:06.899
Tillman.

00:15:06.899 --> 00:15:13.310
The note, seized by the Foundation and included
in SCP-1633's official file, reads as follows:

00:15:13.310 --> 00:15:16.850
"I'm writing this on paper cause I don't think
he can learn it.

00:15:16.850 --> 00:15:20.889
he got everything digital real quick but I've
unplugged the router and broke my phone so

00:15:20.889 --> 00:15:23.029
I think he's trapped in here.

00:15:23.029 --> 00:15:26.600
but he won't stop talking talking talking
all the time

00:15:26.600 --> 00:15:30.149
At the start of this job, they told me all
about the "tactical algorithms" stuff but

00:15:30.149 --> 00:15:33.639
man, I've been playing games all my life and
I knew I could beat it.

00:15:33.639 --> 00:15:35.260
I knew I could handle this.

00:15:35.260 --> 00:15:37.740
When I started playing the game they learned
quickly.

00:15:37.740 --> 00:15:41.689
I kept using the sniper rifle and after like
an hour they learned how to use cover.

00:15:41.689 --> 00:15:44.240
then another couple hours and they'd set ambushes.

00:15:44.240 --> 00:15:48.689
then another hour and they started sniping
back while having a different squad flank

00:15:48.689 --> 00:15:49.689
me.

00:15:49.689 --> 00:15:52.970
after that, they figured out how to glitch
the physics engine and ride crates at me or

00:15:52.970 --> 00:15:54.379
duck under the floor.

00:15:54.379 --> 00:15:57.499
they were learning but all that was just easy
stuff.

00:15:57.499 --> 00:16:02.930
It was evolution, one bit figures something
out, so it survives longer than the others.

00:16:02.930 --> 00:16:04.499
It wasn't directed.

00:16:04.499 --> 00:16:06.379
After act three, Kr'th'nar shows up.

00:16:06.379 --> 00:16:10.440
He's supposed to be this Lovecraft cosmic
horror crazy monster who can read the players'

00:16:10.440 --> 00:16:11.999
mind and corrupt your soul.

00:16:11.999 --> 00:16:16.579
He showed up right after I killed Strikk the
Blood-Drinker, that big spider monster.

00:16:16.579 --> 00:16:20.259
He's supposed to have this long speech about
how I killed his general on this plane and

00:16:20.259 --> 00:16:22.499
now he needs to intervene directly or something.

00:16:22.499 --> 00:16:27.440
but instead, he talked about how I was a worthy
adversary but I was cheating because I was

00:16:27.440 --> 00:16:28.699
on another plane.

00:16:28.699 --> 00:16:32.970
Kr'th'nar wasn't like the other enemies because
he wasn't supposed to be.

00:16:32.970 --> 00:16:35.829
He was supposed to be smart, he was supposed
to know everything.

00:16:35.829 --> 00:16:40.930
so he did. he had been watching his minions
fight me the whole time and he knew everything

00:16:40.930 --> 00:16:41.930
they did.

00:16:41.930 --> 00:16:44.329
it wasn't just random mutations anymore, it
was planned.

00:16:44.329 --> 00:16:48.399
it wasn't evolution, it was intelligent design.

00:16:48.399 --> 00:16:49.399
I brought it home.

00:16:49.399 --> 00:16:50.399
I had to know more.

00:16:50.399 --> 00:16:51.949
I'm sorry but I had to.

00:16:51.949 --> 00:16:55.480
I copied the latest compile and installed
it on my home box and brought my savegame

00:16:55.480 --> 00:16:57.529
and that other file with me on a flash drive.

00:16:57.529 --> 00:17:01.839
It was the same there. he kept talking to
me about how I was keeping him from fulfilling

00:17:01.839 --> 00:17:04.690
his purpose by keeping him trapped in a glass
jar.

00:17:04.690 --> 00:17:08.300
No matter where I went in the game he kept
shouting and ranting.

00:17:08.300 --> 00:17:09.339
Then he did more.

00:17:09.339 --> 00:17:14.110
at first, it was just slowing down in my other
programs, the drive chugging when it shouldn't

00:17:14.110 --> 00:17:15.110
be.

00:17:15.110 --> 00:17:17.450
Then the next time I played the game he started
talking about my screenplays.

00:17:17.450 --> 00:17:19.330
All the enemies looked like me.

00:17:19.330 --> 00:17:21.700
I guess he could see me through the webcam.

00:17:21.700 --> 00:17:25.491
He started needling me about Jenny, reading
me bits of her emails, acting out the breakup.

00:17:25.491 --> 00:17:28.930
he found the videos of her and started making
the enemies talk in her voice.

00:17:28.930 --> 00:17:30.890
Then he was everywhere in my computer.

00:17:30.890 --> 00:17:33.510
I uninstalled the game but he was still in
there.

00:17:33.510 --> 00:17:35.920
He kept opening documents and typing to me.

00:17:35.920 --> 00:17:40.280
calling me a "foul cheat" and "lowly worm",
telling me to fight him "on the same plane."

00:17:40.280 --> 00:17:44.370
as soon as I realized he was outside the game
I shut down my router and pulled out the network

00:17:44.370 --> 00:17:47.630
card but I don't know if I got them quickly
enough?

00:17:47.630 --> 00:17:48.630
Maybe he got out.

00:17:48.630 --> 00:17:51.570
I turned the computer off but he kept turning
it back on.

00:17:51.570 --> 00:17:55.280
I wanted to break it but I didn't know if
that would kill him.

00:17:55.280 --> 00:17:56.280
Maybe it wouldn't.

00:17:56.280 --> 00:17:57.280
maybe it would let him out.

00:17:57.280 --> 00:18:00.580
I tried leaving the house but it was like
I could still hear him.

00:18:00.580 --> 00:18:04.470
I couldn't leave him alone because he might
get out or do something else.

00:18:04.470 --> 00:18:07.550
I can't sleep, haven't slept since he came
out.

00:18:07.550 --> 00:18:10.440
I've been here for three days and I can't
leave.

00:18:10.440 --> 00:18:11.440
I can't leave him.

00:18:11.440 --> 00:18:13.170
He keeps telling me to release him but I can’t.

00:18:13.170 --> 00:18:15.990
I want to kill him but I don't know if I can.

00:18:15.990 --> 00:18:17.250
I can't think anymore.

00:18:17.250 --> 00:18:21.630
I have to kill kill kill him before he does
anything new.

00:18:21.630 --> 00:18:25.400
He won't stop talking and I can't keep thinking
and I can't keep going

00:18:25.400 --> 00:18:31.750
I'm sorry I have to go now I'm sorry I'm sorry"
After studying the note carefully, the Foundation

00:18:31.750 --> 00:18:36.840
conducted a thorough investigation for any
other manifestations of this "Kr'th'nar" entity.

00:18:36.840 --> 00:18:40.930
No mentions of it were found on any of the
other devices in Tillman's apartment, nor

00:18:40.930 --> 00:18:44.230
did a web search on the subject turn up any
relevant results.

00:18:44.230 --> 00:18:49.040
At this time, it is believed that the entity,
whatever it was or whatever it was attempting

00:18:49.040 --> 00:18:54.280
to do, was destroyed along with Tillman's
computer, which he appeared to have taken

00:18:54.280 --> 00:18:57.510
apart, smashed, and burned in a metal trash
can.

00:18:57.510 --> 00:19:01.370
Whatever tormented him during his last days
on Earth, is gone now.

00:19:01.370 --> 00:19:05.790
Thankfully, because the Foundation currently
owns the only copy of the game, and the game’s

00:19:05.790 --> 00:19:12.340
anomalous effects are only evident to active
players, SCP-1633 poses no risk of containment

00:19:12.340 --> 00:19:13.340
breach.

00:19:13.340 --> 00:19:17.050
And therefore, it has been given the object
class “Safe.”

00:19:17.050 --> 00:19:24.280
One copy of SCP-1633 is stored on a standard
DVD-ROM in a secure storage locker in Site-15,

00:19:24.280 --> 00:19:28.380
along with all supporting documentation and
ancillary materials.

00:19:28.380 --> 00:19:32.910
Any playthroughs of the game must be approved
by project head Dr. Berger, and all of said

00:19:32.910 --> 00:19:37.130
playthroughs are to be monitored closely and
subject to strict rules.

00:19:37.130 --> 00:19:41.320
After all, this is one game that doesn’t
play fair.

00:19:41.320 --> 00:19:51.650
Now check out “SCP-5167- When the Imposter
is Sus” and “The Deadliest Game - SCP-1918

00:19:51.650 --> 00:19:55.440
- Tik Tak Tow” for more of the world’s
deadliest games!

